{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And they were a bitterness of spirit.</b> Since it is destined to say: 'If Jacob takes a wife of the daughters of Heth, like these, of the daughters of the land, what do I need life for?' — it teaches you here that they were not agreeable to Isaac and to Rebekah. And furthermore, had Isaac and Rebekah had contentment of spirit from them, they would have dwelt in one house, and when Jacob comes to take the blessings they would inform Isaac: 'This is not Esau your firstborn.' Therefore it teaches you here in advance that they were a bitterness of spirit to Isaac, and he expelled them from his house. [Bitterness of spirit — they were Needling.]" "<b>To Isaac and to Rebekah.</b> Isaac first, according to the words of our Sages — because Rebekah was the daughter of priests, idolatry did not harm her as it did Isaac, the son of saints."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The bracketed annotation מרת רוח מקנתרות explains the phrase via a cognate root meaning 'to needle/provoke'; rendered literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:06:11.018600241Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}